  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...Active weather \ncontinues through the end of the weekend, with potential for a \nbreak Monday and Tuesday. A low pressure system is expected to \nmove into the Gulf of Alaska through Sunday morning, bringing \nwidespread rainfall through Sunday and lingering in the southern \npanhandle Monday morning. There is still some differences between \nmodels on where this low will be in the gulf, though starting to \nlean towards the more southern, closer to the panhandle position. \nAs the low moves in from the west, the northern panhandle should \nsee the front move in first before rainfall quickly spreads to the\nrest of the panhandle through the morning. Overall, still \nexpecting much of the panhandle to see around an inch of rainfall \non Sunday, with closer to an inch and a half for parts of the \nsouthern panhandle and higher elevation areas. Still not expecting\nstrong gulf winds with this system, with coastal waters only \nreaching up to 15 to 20 kts of southeasterly winds with the onset \nof the front before diminishing for next week. As for the inner \nchannels, Lynn Canal may see winds increase as this system moves \nthrough.\n\nSeeing potential for a break going into next week as the low dips \nsouth, though keeping an eye on upper level easterly flow that may \nallow for showers in BC to traverse into the panhandle. The northern \nand central panhandle have potential to see some sun behind this \nfront on Monday, with the southern panhandle joining in by Tuesday. \nThe slight period of ridging that develops over the eastern gulf \nshould allow winds in the inner channels to switch to a more \noffshore direction through Tuesday before the weather becomes more \nactive through the second half of the week. With the potential for \nclearing, sea breezes should increase through midday and turn winds \nback onshore for a period. Daytime high temperatures will also \nincrease above normal for days that see this clearing, allowing for \nhighs reaching 70 degrees across the panhandle Tuesday.",
